---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Colophon — How tzheng.dev is built"
  description="The design and architecture of tzheng.dev: static Astro pages, typed content, local assets and GitHub Pages publishing."
>
  <article class="article-body">
    <a class="text-link" href="/">
      ← Home
    </a>
    <span class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:35px">
      Colophon / decisions behind the site
    </span>
    <h1>
      A small site.
      <br />A complete system.
    </h1>
    <p class="lead" id="origin">
      The site has one job: make the work easy to understand and the evidence
      easy to inspect.
    </p>
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <strong>On this page</strong>
      <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>
      <a href="#content">Content</a>
      <a href="#design">Design</a>
      <a href="#publishing">Publishing</a>
      <a href="#boundaries">Boundaries</a>
    </nav>
    <h2 id="architecture">Build once. Read anywhere.</h2>
    <p>
      Astro generates the pages ahead of time. Projects and articles live in
      typed Markdown collections, while a dated contribution record supplies the
      accepted upstream work. Readers receive HTML and CSS without waiting for a
      database or a client application to fetch the content.
    </p>
    <div class="architecture-flow">
      <div>
        <b>01 / Source</b>
        <span>Astro + Markdown + records</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>02 / Validate</b>
        <span>Types + content + links</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>03 / Build</b>
        <span>Static HTML + assets</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>04 / Publish</b>
        <span>GitHub Pages</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      The same shared layout handles navigation, metadata and the footer across
      the homepage and detail pages. The homepage reads project collections
      directly; it no longer rewrites a legacy HTML page with string
      replacements.
    </p>
    <h2 id="content">One record, several views.</h2>
    <p>
      A project record provides a title, summary, stack, source link and case
      study. The same entry appears on the homepage, in the project index and at
      its own URL. Articles have publication state and dates; drafts do not
      appear in the public archive or feed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Contribution totals are derived from the merged-PR list. The snapshot date
      stays visible because a static record is not a live counter.
    </p>
    <p>
      Individual pages keep canonical URLs, structured identity references, RSS
      and sitemap output. Links point to the actual case study or article rather
      than opening long-form content in a modal.
    </p>
    <span id="interface"></span>
    <h2 id="design">Paper, ink and a little copper.</h2>
    <p>
      The interface uses a warm light background, restrained green and copper
      accents, and Adobe’s Source Serif 4 Display paired with system sans-serif
      and monospace. Typography and dividers establish the hierarchy; the
      portrait keeps the site personal.
    </p>
    <p>
      The font and optimized images are served locally. There are no third-party
      font requests, ambient particle loops or custom cursor effects. Motion is
      limited to a short introduction and interaction feedback, with a
      reduced-motion alternative.
    </p>
    <p>
      The article archive adds a small search-and-filter script. Its complete
      list remains available without JavaScript. Mobile navigation uses native
      disclosure behavior.
    </p>
    <span id="delivery"></span>
    <h2 id="publishing">Source to a verified artifact.</h2>
    <p>
      A push to the main branch runs the existing GitHub Actions workflow. It
      installs from the lockfile, checks the project, builds the static
      artifact, checks local links and uploads the artifact to GitHub Pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      The built-site check covers local routes, images, fragment links, page
      headings and identity references. Framework upgrades also need a visual
      check: valid types cannot prove that a heading wraps well on a phone.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/0xTaoZ/tzheng.dev">
        Inspect the repository and publishing workflow ↗
      </a>
    </p>
    <span id="problems"></span>
    <span id="security"></span>
    <h2 id="boundaries">Complexity follows a reader’s need.</h2>
    <p>
      A visitor can read, follow the source and send an email. That does not
      require an account, contact database, administration panel or public
      application backend.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site does not embed advertising or analytics scripts. Hosting and
      delivery providers can still process connection data; the{" "}
      <a href="/legal/#datenschutz">privacy note</a> describes the public site’s
      current scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the site later needs authenticated collaboration or dynamic data, that
      would be a separate product decision. The current publishing model is
      intentionally static.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
